# terrace — the Dachgarten: a formal garden on the leads, inside the balustrade, round the drum.
#
# Two paved courts east and west of the drum, each with a walk curving round it, four parterres of
# clipped box on gravel, citrus in tubs on the bay spacing, a raised cistern of water at the north
# end and an armillary sphere at the south, a treillage arbour against the outer end, seats, eight
# lanterns and an iron guard rail along the open edges. It stands ON roof.clip's lead deck and
# INSIDE parapet.clip's balustrade and takes nothing away from either.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — four things, and they are the reason it exists.
#
# 1. THE ONLY LARGE OPEN-SKY SURFACE IN THE BUILDING THAT IS NOT THE GROUND. Everything else
#    outdoors here is a wall seen edge-on or the lawn. This is 166 m² of flat floor eleven metres up
#    with a balustrade round it, a dome standing out of it, and nothing above it: the ambient
#    occlusion case in its pure form. The horizon from this floor is a rail 0.70 m high, so the sky
#    term should be very nearly full in the middle of a court and fall away only in the last metre
#    before the parapet — a shallow, wide gradient across a large flat plane, which is exactly the
#    shape of gradient that bands if the sky term is quantised or interpolated over too coarse a
#    grid. If it bands anywhere in this building, it bands here, and it will band in stripes
#    parallel to the parapet.
#
# 2. THIN METAL HOOPS AGAINST THE SKY. The armillary's own rings are 0.032 to 0.050 m through — one
#    to one and a half voxels at the contract's metre 32; only the fixed meridian, at 0.090, is more
#    than two — and they stand free in open sky at 13.53 m with nothing behind them but the
#    brightest thing in the frame. That is the hardest silhouette in the building: unlike a
#    baluster, which at least has a neighbour 0.27 m away to be compared with, a hoop is a single
#    curve of pure thin matter whose own far side is also in the picture. The treillage is the same
#    test at a hundred times the count — 43 bars of 0.06 m to a screen, crossed by 7 more, over a
#    panel of sky 9.90 m by 1.85 m. A sampler that smears either turns the hoop into a smudge and
#    the lattice into a grey panel, and both failures are visible from the lawn.
#
# 3. STILL WATER AGAINST A LIT DOME. Two cisterns of 2.25 x 2.25 m of `water` (ior 1.33, rough 24),
#    0.30 m deep on a lead floor, flat, open to the sky, with the drum and the dome standing over
#    them. A specular reflection of a large lit curved surface in a small flat one, outdoors, with
#    sky behind it — and 0.30 m of a refracting medium over a rough metal floor, which is where the
#    caustic goes. It is the only water in this building that is not indoors.
#
# 4. FOLIAGE, WHICH THIS BUILDING HAS NONE OF ABOVE THE GROUND. Clipped box, citrus crowns and
#    citrus trained over treillage: dense small-scale matter with no flat face anywhere on it, which
#    is the opposite of everything else here and the case where a summary of far geometry has
#    nothing large to hold on to. It is built as real matter — a `cells` field displacing a rounded
#    mass — and not as a green box, so what a summary has to summarise is a real surface with real
#    curvature and not a lie about one.
#
#    AND THE CROWN IS NINE MASSES AND NOT ONE. A tree drawn as a sphere on a stick is a silhouette
#    problem before it is a foliage problem: a sphere is the same shape from every direction, so
#    nothing about its outline tells the eye where the light is, and sixteen identical ones tell it
#    the building is a diagram. The crowns here are nine overlapping ellipsoids apiece with sky
#    between them, over four limbs that divide, on a trunk that tapers because it is a cut cone and
#    not a capsule — and there are two different trees set out A B A B so no two standing together
#    are the same. See THE TREE, below, for what each of the three costs and what it is worth.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT THE ROOF ACTUALLY IS, AND WHY THE PLAN IS TWO COURTS AND NOT ONE ROOM. This is the thing
# worth reading in this file. The brief describes the terrace as one walled room 32 x 15 m with a
# walk all round the drum. Measured off roof.clip and drum.clip, it is not:
#
#   the deck        roof.clip's slab is level only inside |x| <= 14.35 and |z| <= 5.85. Outside that
#                   is THE GUTTER, a channel 1.20 m wide with its sole at 12.00 running the whole
#                   perimeter, with an outlet gully and a bronze grating in the middle of each side.
#   the drum        drum.clip's plinth is an annulus out to 5.85 and its copper apron lip out to
#                   5.895, both topping out level with the deck at 12.15.
#
# So NORTH AND SOUTH OF THE DRUM THERE IS NO GROUND AT ALL: the drum's apron ends at 5.895 and the
# gutter begins at 5.85, and the apron actually oversails the channel by 0.045. A walk round the
# drum would have to be carried over the gutter on a bridge, and the north and south gullies sit at
# |z| 6.55 .. 7.10 on the centre line, which is precisely where such a bridge would want to land. A
# terrace that ponds water is wrong and a terrace laid over the drainage is worse, so THE WALK ROUND
# THE DRUM IS NOT COMPLETED. Each court has its own quarter-circle ambulatory ending north and south
# at the terrace edge, and the two courts do not meet. That is a real fact about this building, like
# the 0.70 m balustrade, and it is left standing rather than papered over.
#
# For the same reason the cistern and the armillary cannot both stand on the building's own
# north-south axis: that axis is drum, then gutter, then gully. They stand instead on the north and
# south cross axis OF EACH COURT — cistern north, dial south, as the brief has them — which keeps
# the pairing, keeps the whole terrace symmetric about x = 0 (rule 3), and puts two of each in the
# scene rather than one, which for both load cases is more of the thing being measured, not less.
#
# THE OTHER THING THE ROOF IMPOSES: THE ROLLS. 26 lead rolls run down the fall at a pitch of 0.90 m
# (D), standing 0.045 m proud. They are the drainage and they are also roof.clip's own thin-matter
# case, so the paving is not allowed to bury them. It does not: the bed carries a channel 0.18 m
# wide and 0.09 m deep over every roll line, cut from the bed and not from the roll, which leaves
# 0.045 m of air either side of each roll and a continuous run of it from the drum to open lead
# beyond the paving. Water under this terrace goes where it went before. The paving edges stand
# BETWEEN roll lines, never on one: |z| = 4.95 is the midpoint of the gap between the rolls at 4.50
# and 5.40, and the roll at 5.40 is left in the open with 0.90 m of bare lead beside it.
#
# NO PART OF THIS TERRACE COMES WITHIN 0.40 m OF A GRATING. The four gullies are at |x| 15.05..15.60
# and |z| 6.55..7.10; the paving stops at 13.95 and 4.95.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, bay = 2.70. Every dimension below is a whole multiple or a simple
# fraction of one of them. The numbers that come from somewhere else, all measured, none invented:
#
#   12.15 12.1125 12.075 12.0375   roof.clip's four bay levels, stepping down at x = 8.05, 10.30,
#                                  12.55. The bed's underside steps with them, 0.05 into each.
#   0.045, 0.90                    the rolls' half width and their pitch.
#   5.895                          drum.clip's copper apron lip. The paving's inner arc stands at
#                                  6.30 (14 M), which clears it by 0.405 and leaves the apron ring
#                                  visible in the lead where roof.clip says it reads.
#   13.00                          parapet_top. The rail tops out at 13.20, 0.20 above it. Nothing
#                                  on this terrace comes nearer the balustrade than 1.87 m in x or
#                                  2.46 m in z; the ground between is roof.clip's, and stays its.
#   1.35, 4.05                     pilaster axes, from the contract's 2.70 bay. Every pot and every
#                                  citrus stands on one of them, so the ranks line up with the
#                                  windows below; the two lantern standards sit on the same bay.
#
# THE LEVELS:
#
#   11.9875 .. 12.10   the bed's underside, stepped per bay, 0.05 (the brief's overlap) into each
#   12.0975 .. 12.21   the top of the roll channels, one height to each bay, each 0.015 clear of
#                      its own bay's roll
#   12.30              THE FLOOR. 11.85 + 0.45 (M above the deck's soffit); 0.15 (M/3) above the
#                      lead at the drum, so the paving reads as a course laid on the roof and not
#                      as the roof. The lid over a roll channel is 0.09 (M/5) on bay one, and
#                      0.1275, 0.165 and 0.2025 on the three bays outside it.
#   12.255             the floor of a paving joint and the top of the four worn hollows: the floor
#                      less 0.045, which is a voxel and a half at metre 32 and NOTHING at metre 8,
#                      where no voxel centre lies between 12.255 and 12.30 at all. That is the test
#                      a piece of detail has to pass here — it may cost the coarse sample nothing.
#   12.45              kerbs, plinths and pot feet: M/3 above the floor
#   12.75              the cistern rim and the seats: M above the floor
#   13.20              the iron rail: 2 M above the floor, 0.20 above the balustrade
#   14.019             the lantern finials, which are the highest bronze
#   14.20              the highest leaf on a tub citrus
#   14.19              the arbour cornice, the highest stone; 14.255 the armillary's meridian ring;
#                      14.22 the highest leaf, which is on the treillage. NOTHING reaches 14.30.
#
# WHY THE FLOOR IS AT 12.30 AND NOT AT 12.25. At 12.25 the lid over bay one's channel is 0.04 —
# one and a quarter voxels at metre 32 and a third of one at metre 8 — and the paving comes apart
# into twelve strips lying between the roll lines and reads as decking. 12.30 leaves 0.09, M/5, the
# same number the entablature's dentils are built on, and it is the first module fraction above the
# rolls that does. The cost is that the balustrade is 0.70 above the floor instead of 0.75, which is
# still low for a person, still deliberate, and still not mine to fix.
#
# WHY THERE IS NO VOID. This part is laid ON a roof, not cut into one. The cistern is a RAISED
# basin — a rim standing 0.45 above the floor with a lead pan inside it — for exactly that reason: a
# sunk basin would need a void, a void would have to be subtracted from the whole building after
# assembly, and the note at the foot of clips/facility.clip is what happens when a room's void is
# written a little wrong. There is nothing here worth that risk, so `part_terrace` is the only
# binding this file exposes and it removes nothing from anybody.
#
# WHAT WAS TRIED AND IS NOT HERE: a treillage BERCEAU — an arbour you walk under — which is what a
# Dachgarten of this date would really have had at the ends. The zone runs 12.30 to 14.30 and the
# head-height rule is 2.10, so a berceau over this floor would have to break one of them by 0.10.
# It is a screen instead, with its cornice kept flush with its own face so nothing overhangs a place
# a person can stand.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE PLAN, from the drum outwards. Everything is drawn once in the quarter x > 0, z > 0 and folded
# twice, so the two courts and the two ends of each court cannot drift apart.
#
#   x  6.30 .. 7.65   the ambulatory, an arc of 3 M round the drum
#      7.65 .. 9.45   parterre panel A, 4 M
#      9.45 .. 11.25  the cross walk, 4 M, on 10.35 (23 M)
#     11.25 .. 13.05  parterre panel B, 4 M
#     13.05 .. 13.50  the verge walk, M
#     13.50 .. 13.95  the treillage screen, M deep. Then 0.40 of open lead to the gutter.
#
#   z  0.00 .. 0.90   the central walk, 2 M
#      0.90 .. 2.25   the parterre panels, 3 M
#      2.25 .. 4.95   the terminal compartment, 6 M: the cistern north, the dial south. Then the
#                     roll at 5.40 in the open and 0.90 of lead to the gutter.
# ================================================================================================

# --- the bed and the paving -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Four boxes, one to each of roof.clip's bay levels, each bedded 0.05 into its own bay, all topping
# out at the floor. The drum's circle is taken out at 6.30, which is what curves the ambulatory.
let terr_bed_one = box  0.00 12.1000 -4.95   8.05 12.30 4.95  round=0.012
let terr_bed_two = box  8.05 12.0625 -4.95  10.30 12.30 4.95  round=0.012
let terr_bed_thr = box 10.30 12.0250 -4.95  12.55 12.30 4.95  round=0.012
let terr_bed_fou = box 12.55 11.9875 -4.95  13.95 12.30 4.95  round=0.012
let terr_bed_all = union { terr_bed_one terr_bed_two terr_bed_thr terr_bed_fou }

# The well the drum stands in, cut generously: 6.30 (14 M) clears the copper apron lip at 5.895 by
# 0.405 and leaves the apron ring lying in bare lead where roof.clip says it reads as a green ring.
let terr_well = cylinder 0 12.60 0  r=6.30 h=1.60 axis=y

# THE ROLL CHANNELS. One slot to a roll line, 0.18 wide (0.045 of air either side of a roll 0.09
# across). Eleven lines, at z = 0 and every 0.90 out to 4.50; the roll at 5.40 is outside the paving
# altogether. They run the whole length of the paving in x and open onto bare lead at both ends, so
# the fall under this terrace is the fall roof.clip laid.
#
# FOUR SETS AND NOT ONE, ONE TO EACH BAY, EACH CUT TO ITS OWN BAY'S ROLL AND NO HIGHER — the roll
# top plus 0.015 of air. That is not fussiness, it was measured. Cut to a single top of 12.21 (which
# is what the tallest roll, on bay one, needs) the lid over the outer three bays is 0.09 everywhere,
# and 0.09 is under a voxel at metre 8: no voxel centre lands in it, the whole paving comes apart
# into twelve strips lying between the roll lines, and the part reports 52 components. Cut per bay
# the lid is 0.09 on bay one and 0.1275, 0.165 and 0.2025 further out, the outer three bays keep a
# continuous course at any detail, and bay one's strips are joined by it. Same channels, same
# drainage, same 0.045 of air either side of every roll — and one component to a court.
let terr_slot_one = repeat { box  3.00 11.90 -0.09    8.05 12.2100 0.09 } z=0.90 nz=5
let terr_slot_two = repeat { box  8.05 11.90 -0.09   10.30 12.1725 0.09 } z=0.90 nz=5
let terr_slot_thr = repeat { box 10.30 11.90 -0.09   12.55 12.1350 0.09 } z=0.90 nz=5
let terr_slot_fou = repeat { box 12.55 11.90 -0.09   14.20 12.0975 0.09 } z=0.90 nz=5
let terr_roll_slot = union { terr_slot_one terr_slot_two terr_slot_thr terr_slot_fou }

# THE FLAGGING. A grid of granite joint bands 0.09 wide on a 0.90 pitch (D), let into the top 0.09
# of the travertine and offset by 0.45 so that a joint never lands over a roll channel. It is
# geometry and not a pattern rule, and it is intersected with my own paving, because a bare pattern
# is true everywhere in the clip and would flag the lawn as readily as this floor.
#
# It goes into the QUARTER and not into the court, so that the two folds put the joints at +-0.45,
# +-1.35 ... either way from both axes. Built at court level the +0.45 offset would run the grid out
# to 4.95 on the north and stop at 4.05 on the south, which is a seam the eye finds at once.
let terr_joint_seed_x = repeat { box -1 12.21 -0.045   20 12.31 0.045 } z=0.90 nz=5
let terr_joint_seed_z = repeat { box -0.045 12.21 -6   0.045 12.31 6 } x=0.90 nx=15
let terr_joint_grid = translate { union { terr_joint_seed_x terr_joint_seed_z } } 0.45 0 0.45

# AND THE JOINT IS A CUT, NOT A STRIPE. The band above painted granite and stopped there, which is
# a drawing of a joint: one flat plane with a pattern on it, and at a low sun it stays one flat
# plane because nothing on it can cast a shadow. The same grid raised by 0.045 and SUBTRACTED sinks
# every joint 0.045 into the floor, so what is left standing between them is 0.90 m slabs with an
# arris each, and the granite band is now the bottom of the joint rather than a stripe across the
# top of the stone.
#
# 0.045 deep and 0.09 wide is one and a half voxels by three at the contract's metre 32. It is
# NOTHING at metre 8 and that is deliberate: the groove floor at 12.255 is above the 12.1875 voxel
# centre that carries the paving there, so no coarse sample loses a single voxel of floor to it and
# the two-component-per-court result the file's own note records is untouched. Detail that costs
# the coarse sample nothing is detail that can be afforded.
let terr_joint_cut = translate { terr_joint_grid } 0 0.045 0

# WHERE FEET FALL, AND WHERE THE FROST GETS IN. Six dishes and five chips, drawn in the quarter
# and folded in z, all of them MATTER TAKEN AWAY and none of them paint:
#
#   the dishes   flattened ellipsoids centred exactly on the floor, so each cuts its own ry into
#                it and no arithmetic is needed to say how deep it is. They stand where a person
#                actually stands — in front of the ambulatory seat, on the cross walk, and at the
#                south kerb of the cistern where you lean over it. 0.040 to 0.050 deep: one and a
#                half voxels, hollowed over three quarters of a metre, which is what a century of
#                feet does to a soft stone and what no paint rule can imitate.
#
#                EACH ONE SITS INSIDE A SINGLE SLAB, centred on a multiple of 0.90 and never wider
#                than 0.80, and that is not composition — it is the only arrangement that does not
#                shed voxels. A dish 1.60 across in front of the seat ran its own shallow rim
#                THROUGH the joint grooves at x = 7.65 and z = +-0.45, and where a rim that is
#                rising 0.05 over half a metre crosses a groove that is 0.045 deep, the stone left
#                between them is one voxel thick and touches its neighbours only at a corner. That
#                one dish put the part from 38 components to 46 at metre 32; six slab-sized ones
#                take out MORE stone in total and the count is 38 with the same 94 loose voxels the
#                armillary has always had. A slab wears in its own middle in any case, because the
#                joint round it is where the foot lands last.
#   the chips    spheres straddling the open arris at |z| = 4.95, the one edge on this terrace with
#                a 0.30 m drop under it and nothing over it. Unequal on purpose.
#   the chamfer  the same arris, taken off at 45 degrees for 0.05 along its whole length. A plane
#                confined to a band box, because a chamfer is a plane and a `wedge` can only cut
#                the other diagonal.
#   the settled  six single slabs taken down 0.030, 0.0375 or 0.045 — one voxel, one and a fifth,
#                one and a half at metre 32. A slab bed is sand and no two bays of it settle the
#                same, so the joint beside a settled slab reads as a STEP rather than as a groove,
#                and that step is the difference between a floor of slabs and a floor with lines
#                ruled on it. Each is a whole 0.90 cell of the joint grid — the grid runs at
#                +-0.45 + 0.90k, so a slab is centred on a multiple of 0.90 — and every one of the
#                six stands where nothing else does: no kerb, foot, plinth or sill is bedded into
#                a slab that moves under it.
let terr_wear_q = union {
    ellipsoid  7.200 12.30 0.000  rx=0.38 ry=0.050 rz=0.38
    ellipsoid  7.200 12.30 0.900  rx=0.36 ry=0.040 rz=0.22
    ellipsoid  9.900 12.30 0.000  rx=0.38 ry=0.045 rz=0.38
    ellipsoid 10.800 12.30 0.000  rx=0.38 ry=0.045 rz=0.38
    ellipsoid  9.900 12.30 1.800  rx=0.36 ry=0.040 rz=0.36
    ellipsoid 10.800 12.30 1.800  rx=0.36 ry=0.040 rz=0.36

    sphere  5.85 12.290 4.95 r=0.075
    sphere  7.20 12.300 4.95 r=0.045
    sphere  8.62 12.300 4.95 r=0.055
    sphere 11.91 12.280 4.95 r=0.065
    sphere 13.44 12.300 4.95 r=0.050

    box  7.65 12.2625 -0.45    8.55 12.35  0.45
    box  9.45 12.2550  1.35   10.35 12.35  2.25
    box  6.75 12.2700  2.25    7.65 12.35  3.15
    box  7.65 12.2625  3.15    8.55 12.35  4.05
    box 12.15 12.2550  3.15   13.05 12.35  4.05
    box  9.45 12.2700 -0.45   10.35 12.35  0.45

    intersection {
        plane 0 -1 -1 at=-12.16224
        box -1 12.20 4.85   14.20 12.36 5.05
    }
}
let terr_wear = mirror { terr_wear_q } axis=z

let terr_paving = difference { terr_bed_all  terr_well  terr_roll_slot  terr_joint_cut  terr_wear }

let terr_joints = intersection { terr_paving terr_joint_grid }

# The stone AROUND the dishes, for the coat that goes on it. A hollow worn by feet is not the same
# colour as the floor beside it — it is burnished rather than weathered, because the one thing that
# never happens in it is that dust settles and stays. The shapes are the dishes again, grown, and
# intersected with the paving so the rule cannot reach anything that is not this floor.
let terr_worn_q = union {
    ellipsoid  7.200 12.30 0.000  rx=0.44 ry=0.115 rz=0.44
    ellipsoid  7.200 12.30 0.900  rx=0.42 ry=0.105 rz=0.28
    ellipsoid  9.900 12.30 0.000  rx=0.44 ry=0.110 rz=0.44
    ellipsoid 10.800 12.30 0.000  rx=0.44 ry=0.110 rz=0.44
    ellipsoid  9.900 12.30 1.800  rx=0.42 ry=0.105 rz=0.42
    ellipsoid 10.800 12.30 1.800  rx=0.42 ry=0.105 rz=0.42
}
let terr_worn = intersection { terr_paving  mirror { terr_worn_q } axis=z }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PARTERRES — box scrollwork on gravel, in a granite kerb
# ================================================================================================
#
# The one place in this building where a curve is drawn in plants rather than in stone. Each panel
# is 1.80 x 1.35 (4 M x 3 M) inside a kerb of 0.09, and carries the same figure: a ring, four corner
# volutes and a ball at the centre, all of it displaced by a `cells` field so that the surface is
# leaf-scale and has no flat face on it anywhere. Drawn once about its own centre and set into both
# panels.

let terr_leaf = cells size=0.105

let terr_par_fig = union {
    torus 0 12.48 0  ring=0.36  tube=0.135 axis=y
    torus  0.54 12.48  0.315 ring=0.135 tube=0.09 axis=y
    torus -0.54 12.48  0.315 ring=0.135 tube=0.09 axis=y
    torus  0.54 12.48 -0.315 ring=0.135 tube=0.09 axis=y
    torus -0.54 12.48 -0.315 ring=0.135 tube=0.09 axis=y
    sphere 0 12.50 0 r=0.24
}
let terr_par_box = displace { terr_par_fig terr_leaf } amount=0.05
let terr_hedge_a = translate { terr_par_box }  8.55 0 1.575
let terr_hedge_b = translate { terr_par_box } 12.15 0 1.575
let terr_hedges = union { terr_hedge_a terr_hedge_b }

# The kerb: a granite frame 0.09 thick standing M/3 above the floor, bedded 0.05 into it.
#
# ROUNDED AT 0.025 AND NOT AT 0.01, and one sphere out of a corner of each. A kerb is the piece of
# this terrace a wheelbarrow hits: its arris is never sharp after the first season, and 0.025 is
# most of a voxel at metre 32 where 0.01 is a third of one and reads as a machined edge. The inner
# box is rounded too, or the outside is worn and the inside is not, which is the wrong way round —
# the inside is where the gravel scours it.
let terr_kerb_a = difference {
    box  7.65 12.25 0.90    9.45 12.45 2.25  round=0.025
    box  7.74 12.25 0.99    9.36 12.50 2.16  round=0.02
    sphere 9.45 12.45 1.62 r=0.055
    sphere 7.65 12.45 2.25 r=0.045
}
let terr_kerb_b = difference {
    box 11.25 12.25 0.90   13.05 12.45 2.25  round=0.025
    box 11.34 12.25 0.99   12.96 12.50 2.16  round=0.02
    sphere 11.25 12.45 1.17 r=0.050
    sphere 13.05 12.45 2.25 r=0.060
}
let terr_kerbs = union { terr_kerb_a terr_kerb_b }

# The gravel, displaced by a cell field so that it is GRAVEL and not a green baize: 0.02 of relief
# at 0.075 m spacing is two to three stones across a voxel at metre 32, which is the coarsest a
# gravel can be and still read as loose. It stops 0.03 under the kerb top, as raked gravel does.
let terr_grit = cells size=0.075 seed=29
let terr_gravel_a = displace { box  7.74 12.28 0.99    9.36 12.42 2.16   terr_grit } amount=0.02
let terr_gravel_b = displace { box 11.34 12.28 0.99   12.96 12.42 2.16   terr_grit } amount=0.02
let terr_gravel = union { terr_gravel_a terr_gravel_b }

# ================================================================================================
# CITRUS IN TUBS — a terracotta pot and a Versailles caisse, each drawn once and set out on the bay
# ================================================================================================
#
# Four to a quarter, sixteen in the terrace. The two on the ambulatory stand on the arc at a radius
# of 6.975 (15.5 M) on the pilaster axes z = 1.35 and 4.05, so their spacing IS the bay and their x
# follows from the circle rather than from a choice. The two on the verge stand at x = 13.275, the
# middle of the M-wide walk, on the same two axes.

# THE POT IS A VESSEL AND NOT A LUMP OF CLAY. The section is the outer wall LESS a core taken out
# from 12.60 up, which leaves a rim 0.06 thick — two voxels at the contract's metre 32 — with earth
# standing in the hollow behind it. Every pot on this terrace is seen from above, because the floor
# it stands on is eleven metres up and the sky is behind it; a pot whose wall has no thickness is
# read as a painted cylinder the moment anybody looks down into it.
#
# THE THROWING RINGS ARE MATTER, AND THEY ARE IN THE SECTION RATHER THAN IN A DISPLACEMENT. Five
# ridges 0.075 apart standing 0.019 proud of the belly, drawn as a `repeat` INSIDE the profile that
# is revolved — so they are exact geometry on an exact surface of revolution, and the sampler is
# never asked to decide whether a voxel is on a bump or beside one.
#
# It was a `displace` by a sine first, and the difference is 48 voxels of loose clay. A sine
# displacement moves the whole surface in and out by 0.018 at a pitch of three voxels; where that
# rides over the grain's own displacement the surface grows bumps whose voxels touch the pot only
# at a corner, and 6-connectivity does not call a corner a join. Measured on one pot at metre 32,
# with the two arms taken back to back:
#
#   the old solid pot                       1 component, nothing floating
#   rings as a displacement, no grain       1 component (plus the probe box's own crop)
#   grain, no rings                         1 component (ditto)
#   BOTH displacements                      7 components, 6 loose voxels — every pot, eight times
#   rings in the section, grain displaced    see below
#
# So the rule this file leaves behind: ONE displacement to a surface, and if a second kind of
# roughness is wanted, cut or build it as geometry. The grain keeps its displacement because
# out-of-round is exactly what a displacement is for — it is the whole surface moving, not a
# feature on it — and 0.02 is above the half-voxel floor at metre 32, so it is not dropped.
#
# THE HOLLOW IS TAKEN OUT AFTER THE DISPLACEMENT AND NOT BEFORE, and that is the other half of why
# this pot survives being sampled. Displacing a shell moves BOTH its faces: a wall of 0.06 with
# 0.038 of displacement on it is 0.06 - 2 x 0.038 at the worst, which is a negative number, and the
# first version of this pot came back at metre 32 as a rim in twenty-two loose pieces. Displace the
# solid, then subtract an exact core, and only the outer face ever moves.
let terr_pot_ridge = repeat { box 0.150 -0.012 -1   0.199 0.012 1 } y=0.075 ny=2
let terr_pot_sect = union {
    box 0 12.42 -1   0.150 12.48 1  round=0.01
    box 0 12.48 -1   0.180 12.78 1  round=0.01
    box 0 12.78 -1   0.210 12.87 1  round=0.01
    box 0 12.87 -1   0.245 12.96 1  round=0.028
    translate { terr_pot_ridge } 0 12.63 0
}
let terr_pot_void = revolve { union {
    box 0 12.590 -1   0.090 12.78 1
    box 0 12.780 -1   0.120 13.01 1
} } axis=y
#
# AND THE SIGN OF A DISPLACEMENT IS THE OTHER HALF OF IT. `displace` adds `amount x pattern` to a
# distance, and a distance is POSITIVE OUTSIDE — so a positive amount over a pattern that is never
# negative EATS the shape, it does not swell it. `abs { grain_broad }` at +0.02 took 0.02 off the
# pot everywhere, which cost the base disc its middle layer at metre 16 and left the whole tub
# standing free of its own plinth in the component list. The same pattern at MINUS 0.02 swells it,
# is out of round in exactly the way a kiln makes a pot out of round, and cannot thin anything:
#
#   +0.02 x |grain|   (erodes)          pot 2 components at metre 32, tub 2 at metre 16
#   +0.02 x grain     (both ways)       pot 2, tub 2 — 193 loose voxels at metre 16
#   no displacement at all              pot 10 components at metre 32 — the bare ridge tips shed
#   -0.02 x |grain|   (swells)          pot 1, tub 1, NOTHING floating at either
#
# The last line is what is here. It is also why the ridges are better off under a swelling than on
# their own: a displacement that only ever adds matter fattens a sub-voxel ridge into something a
# voxel centre can land in.
let terr_pot_turned = revolve { terr_pot_sect } axis=y
let terr_pot_swell  = abs { grain_broad }
let terr_pot_grain  = displace { terr_pot_turned terr_pot_swell } amount=-0.02
let terr_pot_clay   = difference { terr_pot_grain terr_pot_void }
let terr_pot_foot   = box -0.225 12.25 -0.225   0.225 12.45 0.225  round=0.03

# The earth in it, dished as earth in a pot is dished — watered from above and settled 0.03 in the
# middle. It overlaps the wall by 0.035 all round, so the tree in it is standing in the tub and not
# balanced on top of it.
#
# IT COMES UP TO THE RIM AND NOT TO HALFWAY, and that is a sampling fact rather than a gardening
# one. The rim is 0.06 through: at metre 8 a voxel centre lands in it or does not, and where the
# earth stopped 0.055 below the rim the top course of every pot came back as a five-voxel ring
# with nothing under it. Filled to 0.02 under the rim the ring and the earth share their voxels all
# the way down, and the pot is one piece at every detail.
let terr_pot_soil = difference {
    revolve { union {
        box 0 12.605 -1   0.110 12.78 1
        box 0 12.780 -1   0.140 12.94 1
    } } axis=y
    ellipsoid 0 13.000 0  rx=0.16 ry=0.09 rz=0.16
}

let terr_cai_foot = box -0.225 12.25 -0.225   0.225 12.42 0.225  round=0.03
let terr_cai_tub  = difference {
    union {
        box -0.210 12.40 -0.210   0.210 12.87 0.210  round=0.012
        box -0.240 12.87 -0.240   0.240 12.96 0.240  round=0.020
    }
    box -0.150 12.62 -0.150   0.150 13.00 0.150  round=0.02
}
let terr_cai_soil = difference {
    box -0.185 12.60 -0.185   0.185 12.940 0.185
    ellipsoid 0 13.000 0 rx=0.22 ry=0.09 rz=0.22
}

# The ironwork on the caisse, which is what holds a Versailles tub together: four corner straps, a
# band round its waist standing 0.022 proud of the boards, and a bolt through the band into each
# strap. The bolt heads are 0.06 across and stand 0.03 out — one voxel proud, two across, at metre
# 32 — and each is half buried in the strap it fixes, so not one of them is a loose speck.
let terr_cai_strap = union {
    box -0.225 12.44 -0.225  -0.165 12.99 -0.165
    box  0.165 12.44 -0.225   0.225 12.99 -0.165
    box -0.225 12.44  0.165  -0.165 12.99  0.225
    box  0.165 12.44  0.165   0.225 12.99  0.225
    difference {
        box -0.232 12.60 -0.232   0.232 12.66 0.232  round=0.008
        box -0.190 12.58 -0.190   0.190 12.68 0.190
    }
    sphere  0.195 12.76  0.225 r=0.030
    sphere -0.195 12.76  0.225 r=0.030
    sphere  0.195 12.76 -0.225 r=0.030
    sphere -0.195 12.76 -0.225 r=0.030
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE TREE — and the difference between a tree and a lollipop
# ================================================================================================
#
# What stood here was a capsule with a displaced sphere on top of it. Every one of the sixteen was
# the same, and a capsule is a shape with ONE radius by definition, so the trunk could not taper
# even in principle. Three things make it a tree instead, and all three are matter:
#
#   the trunk    a cone cut off at the crotch. 0.23 across where it leaves the earth and 0.16 where
#                the limbs go, which is a real taper and cannot be written as a capsule at all.
#   the limbs    four primaries out of the crotch, each dividing once into two. 0.096 down to 0.052
#                across — three voxels to one and a half at the contract's metre 32 — and every
#                one of them starts INSIDE the trunk, below the top of the cone, so no limb is a
#                separate component at any detail.
#   the crown    nine clumps, none of them concentric with the trunk, the mass carried to one side
#                and one clump hanging low. THE GAPS ARE THE POINT. A single sphere has no
#                silhouette, only an outline: it is the same shape from every side, so nothing
#                about it can tell the eye where the light is coming from. Nine overlapping
#                ellipsoids with sky between them have a lit side, a shaded side, and holes.
#
# TWO TREES AND NOT ONE DRAWN TWICE. The four tubs in a quarter alternate A B A B, so no two trees
# standing together are the same tree, and the fold puts the same pair in the other court. The
# whole crown is displaced ONCE, as a union — nine displaces would be nine slack allowances laid
# over each other for the same 0.06 of leaf.
#
# NOTHING HERE REACHES 14.30. The highest clump tops out at 14.16 and the leaf field takes it to
# 14.22, which is the number at the head of this file and is now set by a tree rather than by the
# treillage.

# The bark is a `rasp` on the TRUNK ONLY and never on the limbs. `rasp` returns -|fbm|, so it only
# ever cuts: 0.02 of gouge into a stem 0.23 across is bark, and the same 0.02 into a twig 0.052
# across is a twig in three pieces. That is the whole reason the trunk and the limbs are separate
# bindings that are unioned at the point of use rather than one shape.
let terr_bark_cut = rasp size=0.045 seed=37
let terr_tree_a_stem = intersection {
    cone 0 12.60 0  r=0.115 h=2.30 axis=y
    box -0.20 12.56 -0.20   0.20 13.30 0.20
}
let terr_tree_a_trunk = displace { terr_tree_a_stem terr_bark_cut } amount=0.02
let terr_tree_a_limbs = union {
    capsule 0 13.10 0        0.26 13.62  0.10  r=0.048
    capsule 0.26 13.62 0.10  0.40 13.86  0.20  r=0.032
    capsule 0.26 13.62 0.10  0.34 13.92 -0.02  r=0.030

    capsule 0 13.12 0       -0.20 13.58 -0.18  r=0.045
    capsule -0.20 13.58 -0.18  -0.34 13.80 -0.28  r=0.030
    capsule -0.20 13.58 -0.18  -0.26 13.88 -0.10  r=0.028

    capsule 0 13.16 0        0.06 13.66 -0.26  r=0.042
    capsule 0.06 13.66 -0.26   0.14 13.86 -0.38  r=0.029
    capsule 0.06 13.66 -0.26  -0.06 13.90 -0.30  r=0.027

    capsule 0 13.20 0       -0.10 13.78  0.16  r=0.038
    capsule -0.10 13.78 0.16  -0.18 13.96  0.26  r=0.026
}
let terr_tree_a_mass = union {
    ellipsoid  0.42 13.84  0.22  rx=0.24 ry=0.20 rz=0.22
    ellipsoid  0.34 13.94 -0.06  rx=0.21 ry=0.18 rz=0.20
    ellipsoid -0.36 13.78 -0.30  rx=0.22 ry=0.18 rz=0.21
    ellipsoid -0.28 13.90 -0.08  rx=0.20 ry=0.17 rz=0.19
    ellipsoid  0.16 13.86 -0.42  rx=0.22 ry=0.18 rz=0.20
    ellipsoid -0.08 13.92 -0.30  rx=0.20 ry=0.17 rz=0.18
    ellipsoid -0.20 13.98  0.28  rx=0.19 ry=0.16 rz=0.18
    ellipsoid  0.04 13.82  0.04  rx=0.26 ry=0.22 rz=0.24
    ellipsoid  0.24 13.56 -0.10  rx=0.18 ry=0.13 rz=0.16
    ellipsoid  0.06 13.40  0.02  rx=0.24 ry=0.20 rz=0.22
}
let terr_tree_a_leaf = displace { terr_tree_a_mass terr_leaf } amount=0.06
let terr_tree_a_crop = union {
    sphere  0.44 13.68  0.24  r=0.065
    sphere -0.34 13.62 -0.32  r=0.060
    sphere  0.18 13.70 -0.44  r=0.060
    sphere  0.26 13.44 -0.12  r=0.055
}

let terr_tree_b_stem = intersection {
    cone 0 12.60 0  r=0.105 h=2.60 axis=y
    box -0.20 12.56 -0.20   0.20 13.38 0.20
}
let terr_tree_b_trunk = displace { terr_tree_b_stem terr_bark_cut } amount=0.02
let terr_tree_b_limbs = union {
    capsule 0 13.18 0       -0.24 13.70 -0.06  r=0.046
    capsule -0.24 13.70 -0.06  -0.38 13.90 -0.16  r=0.030
    capsule -0.24 13.70 -0.06  -0.30 13.94  0.06  r=0.028

    capsule 0 13.22 0        0.18 13.66  0.20  r=0.044
    capsule 0.18 13.66 0.20   0.30 13.84  0.30  r=0.029
    capsule 0.18 13.66 0.20   0.14 13.90  0.14  r=0.027

    capsule 0 13.26 0       -0.04 13.62  0.28  r=0.040
    capsule -0.04 13.62 0.28  -0.10 13.78  0.40  r=0.027

    capsule 0 13.30 0        0.06 13.84 -0.10  r=0.036
    capsule 0.06 13.84 -0.10   0.14 13.98 -0.18  r=0.025
}
let terr_tree_b_mass = union {
    ellipsoid -0.40 13.88 -0.18  rx=0.24 ry=0.19 rz=0.21
    ellipsoid -0.32 13.96  0.08  rx=0.21 ry=0.18 rz=0.20
    ellipsoid  0.32 13.82  0.32  rx=0.23 ry=0.18 rz=0.21
    ellipsoid  0.14 13.92  0.14  rx=0.22 ry=0.20 rz=0.21
    ellipsoid -0.12 13.78  0.42  rx=0.20 ry=0.16 rz=0.19
    ellipsoid  0.16 13.98 -0.20  rx=0.19 ry=0.16 rz=0.18
    ellipsoid -0.02 13.74 -0.06  rx=0.25 ry=0.21 rz=0.24
    ellipsoid -0.14 13.54  0.16  rx=0.17 ry=0.13 rz=0.16
    ellipsoid -0.06 13.48  0.04  rx=0.24 ry=0.20 rz=0.22
}
let terr_tree_b_leaf = displace { terr_tree_b_mass terr_leaf } amount=0.06
let terr_tree_b_crop = union {
    sphere -0.42 13.72 -0.20  r=0.065
    sphere  0.34 13.66  0.34  r=0.060
    sphere -0.14 13.62  0.44  r=0.055
    sphere  0.18 13.82 -0.22  r=0.060
}

let terr_pot_a = translate { terr_pot_clay } 6.843 0 1.35
let terr_pot_b = translate { terr_pot_clay } 5.679 0 4.05
let terr_pots_clay = union { terr_pot_a terr_pot_b }

let terr_cai_a = translate { terr_cai_tub } 13.275 0 1.35
let terr_cai_b = translate { terr_cai_tub } 13.275 0 4.05
let terr_cai_wood = union { terr_cai_a terr_cai_b }

let terr_cai_strap_a = translate { terr_cai_strap } 13.275 0 1.35
let terr_cai_strap_b = translate { terr_cai_strap } 13.275 0 4.05
let terr_cai_straps = union { terr_cai_strap_a terr_cai_strap_b }

let terr_tub_feet = union {
    translate { terr_pot_foot } 6.843 0 1.35
    translate { terr_pot_foot } 5.679 0 4.05
    translate { terr_cai_foot } 13.275 0 1.35
    translate { terr_cai_foot } 13.275 0 4.05
}

# A B A B round the quarter: the ambulatory pair take one tree each and the verge pair take them the
# other way about, so neither court has two of the same tree standing next to each other.
let terr_trunks = union {
    translate { union { terr_tree_a_trunk terr_tree_a_limbs } }  6.843 0 1.35
    translate { union { terr_tree_b_trunk terr_tree_b_limbs } }  5.679 0 4.05
    translate { union { terr_tree_b_trunk terr_tree_b_limbs } } 13.275 0 1.35
    translate { union { terr_tree_a_trunk terr_tree_a_limbs } } 13.275 0 4.05
}
let terr_crowns = union {
    translate { terr_tree_a_leaf }  6.843 0 1.35
    translate { terr_tree_b_leaf }  5.679 0 4.05
    translate { terr_tree_b_leaf } 13.275 0 1.35
    translate { terr_tree_a_leaf } 13.275 0 4.05
}
let terr_fruit_tubs = union {
    translate { terr_tree_a_crop }  6.843 0 1.35
    translate { terr_tree_b_crop }  5.679 0 4.05
    translate { terr_tree_b_crop } 13.275 0 1.35
    translate { terr_tree_a_crop } 13.275 0 4.05
}
let terr_soil = union {
    translate { terr_pot_soil }  6.843 0 1.35
    translate { terr_pot_soil }  5.679 0 4.05
    translate { terr_cai_soil } 13.275 0 1.35
    translate { terr_cai_soil } 13.275 0 4.05
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE TREILLAGE SCREEN — the arbour against the outer end
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.45 deep (M), 1.80 high (4 M) to the head, standing on the paving and bedded 0.05 into it. Oak
# posts every 1.35 with a sill, a head and a projecting cornice, and between them a lattice of bars
# 0.06 x 0.06 — two voxels at the contract's metre 32 — at a pitch of 0.225 (M/2) one way and 0.225
# the other. 42 verticals and 7 horizontals to a screen, twice, against open sky.
#
# The cornice is kept flush with the screen's own face at 13.50 and projects only outwards, so
# nothing overhangs the walk. See the head-height note at the top of this file.
#
# The arrises are taken off at 0.03 and not at 0.012. This is joinery standing outdoors eleven
# metres up: a sill that sheds water has a rounded top, a post that a hand goes round has a
# rounded corner, and 0.012 is a third of a voxel at metre 32 and therefore no edge at all.
let terr_tre_sill = box 13.50 12.25 0    13.95 12.40 4.95  round=0.030
let terr_tre_head = box 13.50 13.92 0    13.95 14.10 4.95  round=0.025
let terr_tre_corn = box 13.50 14.10 0    14.04 14.19 4.95  round=0.025
let terr_tre_post = repeat { box 13.50 12.25 -0.09   13.95 14.10 0.09  round=0.025 } z=1.35 nz=3
let terr_tre_endp = box 13.50 12.25 4.77   13.95 14.10 4.95  round=0.01

let terr_tre_bar_v = repeat { box 13.62 12.35 -0.03   13.74 14.00 0.03 } z=0.225 nz=21
let terr_tre_bar_h = intersection {
    translate { repeat { box 13.62 -0.03 0   13.74 0.03 4.95 } y=0.225 ny=4 } 0 13.1625 0
    box 13.50 12.42 -0.01   13.95 13.92 4.96
}
let terr_treillage = union {
    terr_tre_sill terr_tre_head terr_tre_corn terr_tre_post terr_tre_endp
    terr_tre_bar_v terr_tre_bar_h
}

# The citrus trained over it. Two masses to a screen, growing through the lattice.
let terr_tre_leaf_a = displace { ellipsoid 13.72 13.80 1.35  rx=0.24 ry=0.36 rz=0.72  terr_leaf } amount=0.06
let terr_tre_leaf_b = displace { ellipsoid 13.72 13.05 3.60  rx=0.24 ry=0.45 rz=0.81  terr_leaf } amount=0.06
let terr_tre_leaves = union { terr_tre_leaf_a terr_tre_leaf_b }
let terr_tre_fruit = union {
    sphere 13.72 13.80 2.01  r=0.075
    sphere 13.72 14.10 1.35  r=0.075
    sphere 13.72 13.05 4.35  r=0.075
    sphere 13.72 13.50 3.60  r=0.075
}

# ================================================================================================
# SEATS — granite ends and walnut slats
# ================================================================================================
#
# Two to a court: one with its back to the drum on the ambulatory, one in the arbour on the verge.
# Drawn as the z > 0 half of a bench 1.80 long, so the fold makes the whole of it.
#
# The stone ends are chamfered at 0.03 and one of them has a corner off it; the slats are rounded
# at 0.02 rather than 0.008, which is what forty years of a hand and a coat does to the arris of a
# seat and is two thirds of a voxel at metre 32 instead of a quarter of one. Each slat is bolted
# down through both bearers, and the bolt heads stand 0.025 proud — the ONE place on this terrace
# where the fixings of a thing are at eye level for somebody sitting on it.
let terr_seat_stone = difference {
    union {
        box  6.48 12.25 0.72    6.93 12.72 0.90  round=0.030
        box  6.48 12.25 0.00    6.93 12.72 0.09  round=0.030
        box 13.05 12.25 0.72   13.50 12.72 0.90  round=0.030
        box 13.05 12.25 0.00   13.50 12.72 0.09  round=0.030
    }
    sphere  6.93 12.72 0.90 r=0.055
    sphere 13.05 12.72 0.90 r=0.050
}
let terr_seat_slats = union {
    box  6.510 12.70 0    6.600 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
    box  6.645 12.70 0    6.735 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
    box  6.780 12.70 0    6.870 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
    box 13.080 12.70 0   13.170 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
    box 13.215 12.70 0   13.305 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
    box 13.350 12.70 0   13.440 12.78 0.90  round=0.020
}
let terr_seat_bolts = union {
    sphere  6.555 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere  6.690 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere  6.825 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere 13.125 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere 13.260 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere 13.395 12.775 0.81 r=0.028
    sphere  6.555 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
    sphere  6.690 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
    sphere  6.825 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
    sphere 13.125 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
    sphere 13.260 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
    sphere 13.395 12.775 0.00 r=0.028
}

# ================================================================================================
# LANTERNS — eight of them, on standards along the walks
# ================================================================================================
#
# Two to a quarter and so eight in the terrace: one on the cross walk beside the parterres, one on
# the verge between the two caisses. A bronze standard, a cage of four corner posts with the light
# open between them, and a `lamp` flame standing on the cage floor.
#
# A STANDARD IS BOLTED DOWN, AND A LANTERN HAS A ROOF ON IT. What stood here was a plain tube with
# a cone on the end: nothing said how it was fixed to the floor, nothing shed the rain off the cage,
# and the flame was behind an opening with no bar across it. So, from the bottom: a flange over the
# granite foot with four bolts through it standing 0.03 proud, a congé off the flange into the
# shaft, a knop where a standard is jointed, the hood as it was, a drip ring under its eaves, and a
# spike and ball on top of it. The ball tops out at 14.018 and nothing here reaches 14.30.
let terr_lamp_stem = union {
    cylinder 0 12.90 0  r=0.045 h=1.00 axis=y
    cylinder 0 12.52 0  r=0.090 h=0.14 axis=y
    cone 0 12.59 0  r=0.078 h=0.11 axis=y
    cylinder 0 13.10 0  r=0.062 h=0.06 axis=y
    cone 0 13.71 0  r=0.15 h=0.18 axis=y
    torus 0 13.720 0  ring=0.132 tube=0.024 axis=y
    capsule 0 13.88 0   0 13.95 0  r=0.018
    sphere 0 13.980 0 r=0.038
    sphere  0.068 12.590 0      r=0.030
    sphere -0.068 12.590 0      r=0.030
    sphere  0     12.590  0.068 r=0.030
    sphere  0     12.590 -0.068 r=0.030
}
# The cage: a bottom plate, a top plate and four corner posts of 0.06 — two voxels at metre 32 —
# with the light open between them. The plates are M/5 thick and the openings stop 0.09 short of
# each, and both of those numbers were measured rather than chosen: at 0.03 the plates are half a
# voxel at metre 16, they disappear, and every lamp in the terrace comes back as eight loose specks.
#
# And a glazing bar down the middle of each opening, put back INTO it after it is cut: 0.05 by
# 0.05, running the whole height of the cage so that 0.09 of it is buried in the bottom plate and
# 0.09 in the top. That is what carries the glass in a lantern this size, and it is the bar the
# four corner posts need if the thing is to read as a cage rather than as four posts.
#
# A MULLION AND NOT A RING, AND THE REASON IS THE SAMPLER. The first version of this was a frame
# round the waist of the cage, 0.03 by 0.04, and at metre 16 every one of the eight lanterns came
# back with two loose specks on it: a horizontal bar is sampled where a voxel centre happens to
# land in it, and the ones that land are in the middle of a face, with air above and below and the
# corner posts too far away to touch. A VERTICAL bar cannot do that. Whether a voxel centre falls
# inside it depends on x and z alone, so if it is sampled anywhere in the opening it is sampled at
# every height in that same column, and the column runs into solid plate at both ends.
let terr_lamp_cage = union {
    difference {
        box -0.12 13.35 -0.12   0.12 13.71 0.12  round=0.012
        box -0.06 13.44 -0.30   0.06 13.62 0.30
        box -0.30 13.44 -0.06   0.30 13.62 0.06
    }
    box -0.025 13.35  0.090   0.025 13.71  0.140
    box -0.025 13.35 -0.140   0.025 13.71 -0.090
    box  0.090 13.35 -0.025   0.140 13.71  0.025
    box -0.140 13.35 -0.025  -0.090 13.71  0.025
}
let terr_lamp_foot = box -0.135 12.25 -0.135   0.135 12.45 0.135  round=0.025
let terr_lamp_fire = sphere 0 13.45 0 r=0.10

let terr_lamp_bronze = union {
    translate { union { terr_lamp_stem terr_lamp_cage } } 10.350 0 1.575
    translate { union { terr_lamp_stem terr_lamp_cage } } 13.275 0 2.700
}
let terr_lamp_feet = union {
    translate { terr_lamp_foot } 10.350 0 1.575
    translate { terr_lamp_foot } 13.275 0 2.700
}
let terr_lamp_fires = union {
    translate { terr_lamp_fire } 10.350 0 1.575
    translate { terr_lamp_fire } 13.275 0 2.700
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE RAIL — the terrace's own guard along the open ends
# ================================================================================================
#
# The parapet is not the danger here; it is 2.46 m away across bare lead. The danger is the step off
# this paving at |z| = 4.95 — 0.30 down to the leads and 0.30 more into the gutter, in the dark,
# eleven metres up. So the rail stands on my own paving 0.09 inside its edge, and it tops out at
# 13.20, 2 M above the floor and 0.20 above the balustrade beyond it. The parapet is NOT raised.
#
# The posts are cut to the paving by intersection, which is what removes the two copies of the
# repeat that would otherwise land on bare lead at 3.60 and 14.40. Where the cistern stands the
# posts rise out of its own north kerb, which is what a balustrade does when a basin is set into it.
#
# WHAT A RAIL IS MADE OF, AS OPPOSED TO WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE FROM TWENTY METRES. Three changes, all
# of them section rather than surface:
#
#   the standard   0.060 across the run and 0.075 through it. A stanchion is never square: it is
#                  stiff the way the load comes, which is sideways over the rail, so the deeper
#                  face is the one you see edge-on from along the walk.
#   the fixing     a plate 0.15 square and 0.05 thick bedded into the paving under every standard,
#                  and a collar where the middle bar passes it. Wrought iron is not grown out of
#                  stone; it is bolted to it, and a rail with no plate under it is the detail a
#                  photograph of one never fails to show.
#   the sag        the middle bar is not a straight line. It hangs 0.045 at every midspan and comes
#                  up to the fixing at every standard — one and a half voxels of dip at metre 32,
#                  over 1.35 m, which is what a 0.048 bar spanning that far does.
#
# THE SAG IS TWELVE CAPSULES WRITTEN OUT AND NOT ONE BAY IN A `repeat`, AND THAT COST TWO AND A
# HALF SECONDS TO LEARN. A bay drawn from post to post is 1.35 long and its capsule ends carry a
# radius, so the child of the repeat measures 1.398 across a period of 1.350 — and `metric_slack`
# returns INFINITE for a repeat whose child is wider than its own period, because the fold can then
# hand back a distance to a copy that is not the nearest one. Infinite slack anywhere in an
# expression means the sampler may not skip a single voxel ANYWHERE in the clip: this part went
# from 2.4 s to 5.3 s at metre 8, and every other thing I measured after it was measuring that.
# roof.clip's note about an unbounded child inside a repeat is the same trap wearing a different
# hat. Written out, the whole run is exact and costs nothing.
#
# The top rail stays straight and is a FLAT BAR on edge, 0.036 by 0.060 — a handrail is stiff and a
# hanging one would be wrong. The posts are cut to the paving by intersection, which is what removes
# the two copies of the repeat that would otherwise land on bare lead at 3.60 and 14.40. Where the
# cistern stands the posts rise out of its own north kerb, which is what a balustrade does when a
# basin is set into it.
let terr_rail_post = union {
    box -0.030 12.15  -0.0375   0.030 13.20 0.0375
    box -0.075 12.255 -0.075    0.075 12.33 0.075  round=0.008
    box -0.048 12.690 -0.048    0.048 12.75 0.048  round=0.010
}
let terr_rail_posts = intersection {
    translate { repeat { terr_rail_post } x=1.35 nx=5 } 10.35 0 4.86
    box 4.80 12.20 4.60   13.20 13.30 5.10
}
let terr_rail_sag = union {
    capsule  4.950 12.720 4.86    5.625 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  5.625 12.675 4.86    6.300 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  6.300 12.720 4.86    6.975 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  6.975 12.675 4.86    7.650 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  7.650 12.720 4.86    8.325 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  8.325 12.675 4.86    9.000 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  9.000 12.720 4.86    9.675 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule  9.675 12.675 4.86   10.350 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule 10.350 12.720 4.86   11.025 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule 11.025 12.675 4.86   11.700 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule 11.700 12.720 4.86   12.375 12.675 4.86  r=0.024
    capsule 12.375 12.675 4.86   13.050 12.720 4.86  r=0.024
}
let terr_rail_bars = union {
    box 4.95 13.164 4.830   13.05 13.200 4.890  round=0.008
    terr_rail_sag
}
let terr_rail = union { terr_rail_posts terr_rail_bars }

# ================================================================================================
# THE CISTERN — north end of each court
# ================================================================================================
#
# 2.70 x 2.70 (6 M x 6 M) centred on the court's cross walk at x = 10.35 (23 M), its sides on
# 9.00 (20 M) and 11.70 (26 M). RAISED and not sunk: a granite rim 0.225 (M/2) thick standing 0.45 (M) above the floor,
# a lead pan 0.06 thick inside it, and 0.30 (M/1.5) of water on the lead. No void anywhere.
#
# THE COPING IS THE PART A PERSON TOUCHES, so it is the part with a section on it. The rim is
# rounded at 0.035 outside and 0.025 in — an arris on a basin that has stood outdoors for a century
# is not sharp, and 0.012 is a third of a voxel at metre 32 and no arris at all. Round its waist
# runs a band standing 0.022 proud, a frame and not a slab so it takes nothing out of the water,
# and two corners are off it: the one a barrow reaches and the one under the spout.
#
# The dish in the south coping is where people lean over to look into it. It is 0.035 deep and a
# metre long and it is the same trick as the dishes in the paving — an ellipsoid centred exactly on
# the surface it hollows, so its own ry says how deep it goes.
let terr_cis_rim = difference {
    box 9.000 12.25 2.250   11.700 12.75 4.950  round=0.035
    box 9.225 12.28 2.475   11.475 12.80 4.725  round=0.025
    ellipsoid 10.350 12.75 2.360  rx=0.50 ry=0.035 rz=0.11
    sphere 11.700 12.75 2.250 r=0.075
    sphere  9.225 12.75 4.725 r=0.055
}
let terr_cis_band = difference {
    box 8.955 12.500 2.205   11.745 12.575 4.995  round=0.02
    box 9.045 12.480 2.295   11.655 12.595 4.905
}
let terr_cis_pan = difference {
    box 9.225 12.28 2.475   11.475 12.72 4.725  round=0.02
    box 9.285 12.34 2.535   11.415 12.80 4.665  round=0.02
}
let terr_cis_water = box 9.285 12.34 2.535   11.415 12.64 4.665

# The spout: a bronze block on the north kerb with a nozzle over the water, a collar where the
# nozzle leaves the block, and four bolts holding the block to the stone. The bolts are 0.056
# across and half of each is buried in the block it fixes.
let terr_cis_spout = union {
    box 10.245 12.75 4.680   10.455 13.05 4.950  round=0.02
    capsule 10.35 12.86 4.710   10.35 12.86 4.500  r=0.045
    torus 10.350 12.860 4.690  ring=0.052 tube=0.022 axis=z
    sphere 10.290 12.990 4.680 r=0.028
    sphere 10.410 12.990 4.680 r=0.028
    sphere 10.290 12.800 4.680 r=0.028
    sphere 10.410 12.800 4.680 r=0.028
}

# WHERE THE WATER SITS, FOR THE COAT THAT GOES THERE. Two bands and no geometry: the tide line, a
# 0.09 ring of the pan and the rim's inner face at the water's own surface, and the streak the
# spout has run down the north wall of the basin since it was first turned on. Both are ragged at
# the edge because both are DISPLACED by the shared stone grain before they are painted — a straight
# waterline is a bathtub, and a stain with a machined edge is a decal.
let terr_cis_tide_hard = intersection {
    union { terr_cis_pan terr_cis_rim }
    box 9.10 12.575 2.35   11.60 12.665 4.85
}
let terr_cis_tide = displace { terr_cis_tide_hard grain_stone } amount=0.05
let terr_cis_streak_hard = intersection {
    union { terr_cis_pan terr_cis_rim }
    box 10.185 12.34 4.55   10.515 12.700 4.95
}
let terr_cis_streak = displace { terr_cis_streak_hard grain_fine } amount=0.04

# ================================================================================================
# THE ARMILLARY SPHERE — south end of each court
# ================================================================================================
#
# A granite pedestal, a bronze stem, and the rings: a fixed horizon and meridian of 0.68, and inside
# them the sphere proper — equator, two colures, two tropics and the polar axis — tilted 0.11 turns
# so the axis points at the pole. Tubes run 0.016 to 0.045, which is 0.032 to 0.090 across: one to
# three voxels at the contract's metre 32, and one to one and a half for everything but the
# meridian. THIS IS LOAD 2 AND IT IS THE WHOLE REASON THE PIECE IS HERE. Do not thicken a tube.
#
# The rotate is intersected with a box immediately afterwards, and that is not tidiness: `rotate`
# is deliberately given no bounding box, and a subtree with no box makes every box above it
# unsettleable. The intersection hands one back. (field.cpp, and the note in roof.clip about what
# an unbounded child inside a repeat cost that file.)
#
# Everything touches: the polar rod's ends lie exactly on the meridian ring, because a line through
# the centre of the yz plane meets a circle of that plane whatever its angle; the colures meet the
# equator at their poles; the meridian meets the horizon at z = +-0.68; and the stem meets the polar
# rod at the centre and the pedestal at its foot.
let terr_arm_rings = union {
    torus 0 0 0  ring=0.60 tube=0.025 axis=y
    torus 0 0 0  ring=0.60 tube=0.022 axis=x
    torus 0 0 0  ring=0.60 tube=0.022 axis=z
    torus 0  0.2325 0  ring=0.5535 tube=0.016 axis=y
    torus 0 -0.2325 0  ring=0.5535 tube=0.016 axis=y
    capsule 0 -0.72 0   0 0.72 0  r=0.018
}
let terr_arm_tilt = intersection {
    rotate { terr_arm_rings } x=0.11
    box -0.78 -0.78 -0.78   0.78 0.78 0.78
}
let terr_arm_frame = union {
    torus 0 0 0  ring=0.68 tube=0.030 axis=y
    torus 0 0 0  ring=0.68 tube=0.045 axis=x
}
let terr_arm_gilt = translate { union { terr_arm_tilt terr_arm_frame } } 10.35 13.53 -3.60

let terr_arm_stone = difference {
    union {
        box  9.900 12.25 -4.050   10.800 12.45 -3.150  round=0.030
        box 10.125 12.45 -3.825   10.575 12.90 -3.375  round=0.025
        box 10.050 12.90 -3.900   10.650 13.05 -3.300  round=0.030
    }
    sphere 10.800 12.45 -3.600 r=0.060
    sphere 10.050 13.05 -3.300 r=0.050
}
let terr_arm_stem = capsule 10.35 12.85 -3.60   10.35 13.53 -3.60  r=0.045

# ================================================================================================
# THE MATERIALS, EACH FOLDED TWICE
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every material is collected before it is folded, and every one is folded the same way: what was
# drawn in the quarter is folded in z to make the court, what belongs to one end of a court only is
# added at court level, and the whole court is folded in x. That is what makes the paint match the
# stone exactly — there is no shape in this part that is not in one of these sixteen bindings, and
# no binding that reaches outside this part.

let terr_travertine = mirror { terr_paving } axis=x
let terr_worn_m     = mirror { terr_worn } axis=x

let terr_granite_q = union { terr_kerbs terr_tub_feet terr_lamp_feet terr_seat_stone terr_joints }
let terr_granite   = mirror {
    union { mirror { terr_granite_q } axis=z   terr_cis_rim terr_cis_band terr_arm_stone }
} axis=x

let terr_gravel_m  = mirror { mirror { terr_gravel } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_boxwood_m = mirror { mirror { terr_hedges } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_clay      = mirror { mirror { terr_pots_clay } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_oak       = mirror { mirror { union { terr_treillage terr_cai_wood } } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_walnut    = mirror { mirror { terr_seat_slats } axis=z } axis=x

let terr_iron = mirror {
    mirror { union { terr_rail terr_cai_straps terr_seat_bolts } } axis=z
} axis=x

# The bark carries the earth in the tubs with it. Soil is not a material this building owns and it
# does not need one: `bark` is 88,70,54 at roughness 246, which is wet leaf mould exactly, and the
# trunk standing in it is the same colour for the same reason.
let terr_bark   = mirror { mirror { union { terr_trunks terr_soil } } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_citrus = mirror { mirror { union { terr_crowns terr_tre_leaves } } axis=z } axis=x
let terr_orange = mirror { mirror { union { terr_fruit_tubs terr_tre_fruit } } axis=z } axis=x

let terr_bronze = mirror {
    union { mirror { terr_lamp_bronze } axis=z   terr_cis_spout terr_arm_stem }
} axis=x
let terr_flame = mirror { mirror { terr_lamp_fires } axis=z } axis=x

let terr_lead_m = mirror { terr_cis_pan } axis=x
let terr_water  = mirror { terr_cis_water } axis=x
let terr_gilt_m = mirror { terr_arm_gilt } axis=x

# --- and the three places that are not a material but a state of one ---------------------------
#
# WHERE WATER SITS AND WHERE IT DOES NOT DRY. All three are an intersection of something already in
# this part with a band or a level, so none of them can reach a voxel that is not mine, and two of
# them are displaced by the shared grain first so their edges are ragged rather than ruled.
#
#   the tide     the waterline in both cisterns, and the streak below the spout
#   the foot     the bottom 0.20 of every terracotta pot, which stands in its own drip ring on a
#                floor that does not drain
#   the joints   the granite of the paving joints and the foot of every kerb and plinth, below
#                12.34 — the one part of a baking roof that is damp at eight in the morning
let terr_tide_m   = mirror { union { terr_cis_tide terr_cis_streak } } axis=x
let terr_clay_wet = intersection {
    terr_clay
    displace { box -20 12.40 -20   20 12.60 20   grain_stone } amount=0.05
}
#
# The joints are the only one of the three written as a THRESHOLD rather than as a displaced band,
# and the reason is measured. A band displaced by the grain still paints wherever it comes within a
# few centimetres of a joint, and a joint is 0.045 deep and 0.09 wide: every version of it, at every
# height from 12.18 to 12.30, painted half the joints in the terrace — 2497 voxels at metre 8, 6.7%
# of the part, which is a green floor and not a mossy one. Written as `max(granite, band, 0.15 -
# grain)` the coverage is set by the threshold and nothing else, and 0.15 comes out at 468 voxels,
# which is one joint in four carrying moss and the other three clean.
#
# It costs what an unsettleable rule costs: the fbm has no distance in it, so this one is asked per
# solid voxel inside the granite's box instead of once per block. That is the trade, it was taken
# deliberately, and it is why there is one of these and not three.
let terr_joint_wet = intersection {
    terr_granite
    box -20 12.10 -20   20 12.30 20
    negate { add { grain_broad  constant -0.15 } }
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE PART
# ================================================================================================
#
# It removes nothing from anybody. There is no `void_terrace`: this is laid on a roof, not cut into
# one, and the basins are raised rather than sunk precisely so that it stays that way.

let part_terrace = union {
    terr_travertine terr_granite terr_gravel_m terr_boxwood_m
    terr_clay terr_oak terr_walnut terr_iron
    terr_bark terr_citrus terr_orange
    terr_bronze terr_flame terr_lead_m terr_water terr_gilt_m
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE SURFACE
# ================================================================================================
#
# below=0.02 on every rule and never below=0: the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of
# grain at the end, and a voxel that exists only because the grain pushed the surface outward lies
# OUTSIDE the shape its rule names. At below=0 those voxels match nothing, fall back to the base
# coat, and leave a pale rind over every outward face of this terrace.
#
# Travertine first, because the paving is the largest thing here and everything else is set on it.
paint travertine where=part_terrace  below=0.02

# The hollows the feet made, BEFORE the joints, so a joint running through a worn patch stays a
# joint. Marble and not travertine: what feet do to a soft stone is polish it — the dust that
# weathers the rest of this floor is the first thing that is walked off a tread — so the worn stone
# is paler and smoother than the stone beside it. rough 140 against 228, at 3.1 cm, in the four
# places on this terrace a person actually stands.
paint marble     where=terr_worn_m   below=0.02

paint granite    where=terr_granite  below=0.02
paint gravel     where=terr_gravel_m below=0.02
paint boxwood    where=terr_boxwood_m below=0.02
paint terracotta where=terr_clay     below=0.02
paint oak        where=terr_oak      below=0.02
paint walnut     where=terr_walnut   below=0.02
paint iron       where=terr_iron     below=0.02
paint bark       where=terr_bark     below=0.02
paint citrus     where=terr_citrus   below=0.02
paint orange     where=terr_orange   below=0.02
paint bronze     where=terr_bronze   below=0.02
paint lead       where=terr_lead_m   below=0.02
paint water      where=terr_water    below=0.02
paint gilt       where=terr_gilt_m   below=0.02
paint lamp       where=terr_flame    below=0.02

# --- and last, the three coats that are water rather than material ------------------------------
#
# These go on after every material and before the weathering, because a tide line is a thing that
# happened TO the lead and the granite and must cover both, and because the weathering that follows
# is scoped to the paving and the four shaded corners and will not touch any of them.
#
# `verde` is a stone in the contract's list — 62,92,74 at roughness 180 — and it is here as the
# only green in the building dark enough to read as algae rather than as leaf. Nothing else in the
# list is: `hedge` and `boxwood` are foliage colours, `malachite` is polished and bright, and
# `copper` is the verdigris of a roof, which is a different green and belongs to drum.clip.
paint verde      where=terr_tide_m    below=0.02
paint verde      where=terr_clay_wet  below=0.02
paint verde      where=terr_joint_wet below=0.02

# --- weathering ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The most exposed surface in the building, and the two coats it wants are opposite: sun on the open
# floor, damp in the corners the sun does not reach.
#
# BOTH ARE SCOPED WITH on=<one of my own shapes>, and the shapes are the FOLDED ones, so what is
# weathered is the terrace and nothing else. Without `on=` a coat is keyed on a value rather than on
# a place and goes on after everything anybody painted — it would bleach the lawn 12 m below, scour
# the volutes and sand the steps, which is why the last facility shipped with none.
#
# The damp scope is deliberately small: the two corners of each court where the ambulatory meets the
# terminal compartment, and the two outer corners behind the arbour. Those are the four places on
# this terrace that are in shadow for most of the day — the drum takes the first pair and the screen
# takes the second — and they are the only places moss belongs on a roof that otherwise bakes.
#
# WHAT THESE TWO LINES COST, MEASURED BOTH WAYS, BECAUSE roof.clip FOUND SOMETHING HERE AND ITS
# NOTE NAMES A CAUSE THAT TURNS OUT TO BE ONLY HALF RIGHT.
#
# roof.clip records `weather overgrown` on its gutter taking that part from 110 ms to 17,646 ms at
# metre 8 — 160x — and says the cost is not the scope but `occlusion`, `curvature` and `facing`
# being asked of the whole clip once per solid voxel. This part carries two coats. Three alternated
# pairs of runs with the two lines and with them commented out, at metre 8:
#
#   with    22.9 s   24.3 s   17.1 s
#   without  3.4 s    1.9 s    2.8 s
#
# So a coat here costs about thirteen seconds at metre 8, and two of them cost about eight times
# what the rest of this part costs to sample. I then tried the obvious fix — replacing the scope
# with one box less one cylinder instead of `terr_paving`, which is four boxes less a cylinder less
# forty-four repeated slots, on the theory that the scope's own field is walked per voxel:
#
#   with a scope of 2 nodes    29.4 s   28.6 s   30.2 s
#   without                     2.3 s    2.7 s    1.9 s
#
# IT IS NOT THE SCOPE. A scope of two nodes costs the same as a scope of forty-eight, or a little
# more. roof.clip's diagnosis stands and this is a second, independent measurement of it on a
# different part with a different shape — which is worth more than agreeing with it would have been,
# because the cheap scope was my theory and the control killed it.
#
# The coats stay, because this is the most exposed surface in the building and the brief asks for
# them, and because the cost is a sampling cost paid once and not a frame cost. But nobody should
# add a third here until `weather` stops asking the whole clip for its occlusion. Written up in
# clips/facility/requests/terrace.md.
let terr_damp_q = intersection {
    terr_paving
    union {
        box 3.60 12.10 3.60    6.75 12.40 4.95
        box 12.60 12.10 3.60  13.95 12.40 4.95
    }
}
let terr_damp = mirror { mirror { terr_damp_q } axis=z } axis=x

weather desert    0.05 scale=1.30 seed=61 on=terr_travertine
weather overgrown 0.06 scale=0.70 seed=67 on=terr_damp
